<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trocmi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Trocmi were one of the three Celtic tribes of Galatia in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tolistobogii">Tolistobogii</a> and the Tectosages. After crossing into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> in 278 BCE they settled the eastern part of Galatia, beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kizilirmak">Halys</a> river around Tavium. Each tribe was divided into four tetrarchies, and their lands passed to the Roman province of Galatia in 25 BCE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> and other Greek writers call them Trokmoi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> and Pliny Trogmi and Cicero Trocmi. The name is probably Celtic but its origin is unclear; a link with Old Irish tróg, miserable, has been suggested. They came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> as the last stage of the movement of Celtic peoples from the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>. After the Celtic attacks on Macedonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, the warrior bands were brought across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> through an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedes-i-of-bithynia">Nicomedes I of Bithynia</a>, who wanted them as allies against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>. A tradition reported by Strabo from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/posidonius">Posidonius</a> that derived them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> is rejected by modern scholars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By the end of the 260s BCE the Galatian groups were settled, the Trocmi in the easternmost lands, along the right bank of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kizilirmak">Halys</a>, now the Kızılırmak, beside Pontus and Cappadocia. The local population was neither wiped out nor driven away and probably remained the majority. Their centre, Tavium, had been a place of worship since Hittite times, when it was called Tawinija, and later held a sanctuary of Zeus Tavianos; Posdala was another of their towns.</p><p>The twelve tetrarchs of the three peoples met in council at the Drunemeton. In the mid-1st century BCE the Trocmian tetrarch was Brogitarus, husband of Adobogiona, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deiotarus">Deiotarus</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tolistobogii">Tolistobogii</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>'s settlement of the East in 63 BCE he received Mithridatium, a fortress in Pontus, and land in Armenia Minor, and about 58 BCE Clodius had him made king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, with control of Pessinus. After his death in the late 50s BCE the tetrarchy passed to Deiotarus, apart from a brief recovery by Brogitarus' nephew Mithridates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> from about 47 to 45 BCE.</p><p>Under Augustus, Tavium, until then a fortified place rather than a city, became an autonomous polis and one of the three centres of the provincial league with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ancyra</a> and Pessinus. Its coins of the 1st and 2nd centuries CE name the community the Sebastēnoi Trokmoi Tavianoi. A late Hellenistic cemetery at Boğazköy in their territory has given stone cist graves, jar burials of children, a La Tène fibula and an iron sword with its scabbard.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trocmi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Three tribes, the Tolistobogii, Tectosages and <b>Trocmi</b>, each split into four parts under tetrarchs, met in a council of 300 at a sacred grove called Drynemeton.</blockquote><a href="/state/galatia">Galatia</a></li></ul></section>
